      "math_academy_id": "q-80828",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=3{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}={c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5({c}_{2}-2{c}_{1})+2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-5{c}_{2}+10{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{3} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
